PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Most colleagues converge on B (exactly 1 guaranteed, tight via n-PD). Core arguments: (i) exhaustive 2-player symmetric bimatrix case analysis shows that if both diagonal profiles fail to be NE then both off-diagonal profiles become NE (contradiction to zero); (ii) this lifts to general n by grouping profiles by # of players choosing action 1 (k=0..n) and observing that best-reply transitions on the chain {0..n} must hit at least one stable k; (iii) symmetry maps payoffs only to own action + count of opponents, so unanimous profiles or single-deviator profiles cannot all be unstable simultaneously. Explicit constructions realize exactly 1 (dominant-action game).  
Minority claims for A rely on cyclic/anti-coordination payoffs (odd n, generalized RPS or mismatch matrices) but contain errors: either the game ceases to be symmetric once payoffs are written out, or the claimed absence of NE contradicts the counting argument above. No support for C–E; larger lower bounds are falsified by the PD example.  
Unresolved: none material; the counting argument appears decisive.}

